From: Andrew Hunt <achunt@bellsouth.net>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 13:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Do not send close() for a HAL we never opened

Some vendor Bluetooth HALs assume close() can only ever follow a successful
initialize(), and dereference callbacks that were never installed. Pixel 6a
(bluejay) is one: android.hardware.bluetooth-service.bcmbtlinux aborts with
std::bad_function_call inside BluetoothHci::close():

  bthal.aidl.bcmbtlinux: BluetoothHci::close()
  F DEBUG: >>> /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.bluetooth-service.bcmbtlinux <<<
    #02 std::__1::__throw_bad_function_call()
    #03 android::hardware::bluetooth::aidl::bcmbtlinux::BluetoothHci::close()+984
    #04 android.hardware.bluetooth-V1-ndk.so IBluetoothHci_onTransact
  init: Service 'bcmbtlinux' received SIGABRT

This is fatal rather than cosmetic, because binder_remote_died() reconnects
and then calls configure_bt(proxy, FALSE) on the fresh instance - which has
not been initialized either. So the close kills that one too, and bluebinder
spins in a crash/reconnect loop forever, never bringing Bluetooth up at all.

Track whether the HAL is open, separately from bluetooth_hal_initialized:
_initialized only goes TRUE once the HAL delivers initializationComplete,
whereas the HAL must be closed from the moment initialize() is accepted. Skip
close() when not open, and clear the flag on remote death before reconnecting.

Also gate the shutdown path on the new flag, so a HAL that was opened but
whose init never completed is still closed on the way out.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/bluebinder.c b/bluebinder.c
index c6724d7..3a79642 100644
--- a/bluebinder.c
+++ b/bluebinder.c
@@ -246,6 +246,15 @@ struct proxy {
 
     bool bluetooth_hal_initialized;
 
+    /*
+     * TRUE from the moment initialize() is accepted by the HAL until close()
+     * has been sent. Distinct from bluetooth_hal_initialized, which only goes
+     * TRUE once the HAL delivers initializationComplete - we must not send
+     * close() for a HAL we merely opened but whose init never completed, and
+     * equally must not send it for a HAL we never opened at all.
+     */
+    bool bluetooth_hal_open;
+
     int death_id;
 
     GList *pending_packets;
@@ -450,6 +459,8 @@ configure_bt(
         if (status != GBINDER_STATUS_OK) {
             fprintf(stderr, "ERROR: init reply: %p, %d\n", reply, status);
             fail = TRUE;
+        } else {
+            proxy->bluetooth_hal_open = TRUE;
         }
 
         gbinder_remote_reply_unref(reply);
@@ -457,8 +468,27 @@ configure_bt(
     } else {
         GBinderRemoteReply *reply;
 
+        /*
+         * Never send close() for a HAL we have not opened.
+         *
+         * Some vendor HALs assume close() can only follow a successful
+         * initialize() and dereference callbacks that were never installed.
+         * Pixel 6a's android.hardware.bluetooth-service.bcmbtlinux aborts with
+         * std::bad_function_call inside BluetoothHci::close(), which init then
+         * restarts - and since binder_remote_died() reconnects and calls us
+         * again with bluetooth_on=FALSE, that turns into an endless
+         * crash/reconnect loop that never lets Bluetooth come up at all.
+         */
+        if (!proxy->bluetooth_hal_open) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "Bluetooth already off (HAL not open), "
+                "skipping close\n");
+            proxy->bluetooth_hal_initialized = FALSE;
+            return;
+        }
+
         fprintf(stderr, "Turning bluetooth off\n");
         proxy->bluetooth_hal_initialized = FALSE;
+        proxy->bluetooth_hal_open = FALSE;
 
         reply = gbinder_client_transact_sync_reply(proxy->binder_client,
             proxy->protocol->tx_close, NULL, &status);
@@ -675,6 +705,15 @@ binder_remote_died(
 
     fprintf(stderr, "Remote has died, trying to reconnect...\n");
 
+    /*
+     * Whatever state the old HAL process was in died with it. Any instance we
+     * reconnect to is a brand new process that we have not initialized, so it
+     * is not open - clear this BEFORE reconnecting, because the reconnect path
+     * below finishes by calling configure_bt(proxy, FALSE).
+     */
+    proxy->bluetooth_hal_open = FALSE;
+    proxy->bluetooth_hal_initialized = FALSE;
+
     gbinder_client_unref(proxy->binder_client);
     proxy->binder_client = NULL;
 
@@ -1249,7 +1288,13 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
         g_main_loop_unref(proxy.loop);
     }
 
-    if (proxy.bluetooth_hal_initialized) {
+    /*
+     * Gate on _open rather than _initialized: if initialize() was accepted but
+     * initializationComplete never arrived, the HAL is still open and does
+     * need closing on the way out - and if we never opened it, closing would
+     * crash HALs like bcmbtlinux (see configure_bt).
+     */
+    if (proxy.bluetooth_hal_open) {
         fprintf(stderr, "Turning bluetooth off on stop.\n");
         reply = gbinder_client_transact_sync_reply
             (proxy.binder_client, proxy.protocol->tx_close, NULL, &status);
